    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d423">

                <pb facs="0858" n="828" sameAs="frus:frus1947v01/pg828"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.50 Recovery/7–2447: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Under Secretary of State
                        for Economic Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Clayton</hi>), <hi
                        rendition="#italic">in Geneva</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d423-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-07-24T18:00:00-04:00">July 24, 1947—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>880. For Clayton. Kirk reports<anchor n="2" xml:id="d423-f2"/> that when he told
                    Spaak of Alphand’s<anchor n="3" xml:id="d423-f3"/> reported desire to list
                    colonial resources among assets of <pb facs="0859" n="829" xml:id="pg829"/>
                    nations participating Paris Conference,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d423-f4"/> Spaak
                    assumed that Congo uranium should not be so listed. Kirk informed him, and Dept
                    concurs, that it should not be so included and should if possible not even be
                    mentioned.</p>
                <p>If in any future conversations with any representatives of participating nations
                    you should be asked for suggestions on Conference treatment of radioactive
                    minerals, or if you should hear of any plans for consideration of the subject by
                    participating nations, I hope you can discreetly head off any further
                    development along this line. Dept strongly unwilling link uranium supply or
                    atomic energy development with question of European rehabilitation. Subject is
                    not appropriately within scope of present plans and discussions since industrial
                    application of atomic energy is considered to be still remote.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d423-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d423-f1-content">
                <p>Under Secretary of State William L. Clayton was in Geneva for the Second Session
                    of the Preparatory Committee of the United Nations Conference on Trade and
                    Employment; for documentation on United States participation in that conference,
                    see <ref target="#pg909">pp. 909</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d423-f2" n="2" xml:id="d423-f2-content">
                <p>Reference is to telegram 1134 from Brussels, July 18, not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d423-f3" n="3" xml:id="d423-f3-content">
                <p>Hervé Alphand, Director of Economic Services, French Ministry of Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d423-f4" n="4" xml:id="d423-f4-content">
                <p>For documentation on United States interest in the Paris Conference of the
                    Committee of European Economic Cooperation, see <ref target="frus1947v03#pg_249"
                        >vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 249</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
